WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:04.280
Just like The Mandalorian's inaugural season,
chapters nine through 16 are peppered with

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:06.970
dialogue culled from deep dives into Star
Wars lore.

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:09.730
Here are just some of the lines that mean
more than you realized.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:14.180
But a quick word of warning before we begin,
a galaxy of spoilers are ahead.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:18.080
When Cobb Vanth meets Mando in chapter nine,
he's clearly hoping things won't get violent.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:22.070
In fact, he almost instantly orders, quote,
"two snorts of spotchka" for them.

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:25.980
The Weequay bartender then hands over a bottle
of blue liquid and a couple of cups.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:29.830
While Cobb takes a drink, Mando never gets
to partake in the beverage, but it's still

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:32.590
an interesting allusion to the show's premiere
season.

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:35.930
Season one's chapter four is the first time
that spotchka is mentioned in the Star Wars

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:36.930
universe.

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:40.100
It's a blue drink made from tiny aquatic creatures
called krill.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.960
The krill represent the livelihood of the
same village Mando and Cara Dune help protect

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:45.320
from nearby raiders.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:49.379
The callback is likely intentional, considering
Mando finds himself in a similar situation

00:00:49.379 --> 00:00:54.409
in chapter nine by helping a small community
to organize and eliminate a big threat.

00:00:54.409 --> 00:00:58.639
Chapter nine ends with one of the most long-awaited
reveals in Star Wars history: The return of

00:00:58.639 --> 00:01:02.620
Boba Fett, and consequently, the confirmation
he survived his drop into the Great Pit of

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:04.379
Carkoon in Return of the Jedi.

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:08.390
In a commentary track for the film, George
Lucas said that he'd considered adding a scene

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:10.440
to show the bounty hunter had survived.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:14.960
Also, in Star Wars books no longer considered
canon, it's explained that Boba escaped and

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:16.210
is still alive.

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:20.109
While in The Mandalorian, Boba doesn't reveal
how he survived the sarlacc, it's possible

00:01:20.109 --> 00:01:24.189
an exchange between Mando and Cobb in chapter
nine could give us a hint.

00:01:24.189 --> 00:01:28.070
When Mando, Cobb, and the Tusken Raiders first
arrive at the krayt dragon's lair, Mando tells

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:31.050
Cobb the beast lives in an abandoned sarlacc
pit.

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:34.530
Cobb responds that he's lived on Tatooine
his whole life and there's no such thing as

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:36.570
an abandoned sarlacc pit.

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:39.479
Mando quickly retorts that there is if you
eat the sarlacc.

00:01:39.479 --> 00:01:43.749
It seems unlikely the Great Pit of Carkoon
is the abandoned pit we see in chapter nine.

00:01:43.749 --> 00:01:47.219
The terrain is a lot different from the scene
featuring the beast in Return of the Jedi.

00:01:47.219 --> 00:01:51.610
Still, the revelation that there is a creature
on Tatooine capable of eating sarlaccs gives

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:54.990
us one possible explanation for Boba's survival.

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:58.670
In the beginning of chapter nine's final battle
with the krayt dragon, Mando utters something

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:00.369
that sounds like a curse.

00:02:00.369 --> 00:02:04.590
As the beast begins to back up into his lair,
Mando says, "Dank farrik, it's going back

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:05.590
in."

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:08.810
It's an expression that's repeated throughout
the rest of season two by Mando and other

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:09.810
characters.

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:10.810
"Dank farrik!"

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:14.820
While we don't know exactly what the curse
means, it's a callback to the very beginning

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:16.970
of The Mandalorian's first episode.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:20.830
When we initially hear the phrase, it's spoken
by Mando's first bounty in the series: The

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:23.710
blue skinned Mythrol Mando captures in a bar.

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:26.960
Shortly after he and his prisoner arrive at
the Razorcrest, the ferry that brought them

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:31.270
across the ice is devoured by a monstrous
ravinak and looks like Mando's ship is its

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:32.440
next target.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:37.290
As the pair quickly board the craft, the Mythrol
yells out, "Dank farrik, that was close!"

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:41.010
It's interesting that Mando is now repeating
lines from a former bounty, though he doesn't

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:43.700
quite find himself in the same situation as
the Mythrol.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:47.580
It always seems like Mando can't take a single
step forward on his journey without doing

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:50.910
yet another job for someone else first, and
chapter 10 is no different.

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:55.840
After his adventures in and around Mos Pelgo,
Mando returns to Mos Eisley to find another

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:59.080
lead on Mandalorians and a task he has to
perform for the intel.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:03.511
The mechanic Peli Motto introduces Mando to
someone known only as "the Frog Lady" who

00:03:03.511 --> 00:03:06.220
knows where a group of the elusive warriors
live.

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:09.050
But she wants a trip back home in return for
the information.

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:12.750
As she's giving Mando the low-down on what
she knows, Pelli translates for him

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:17.910
"She said her husband has settled on the estuary
moon of Trask in the system of the gas giant

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:18.910
Kol Iben."

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:22.690
The names of both the moon and the gas giant
are references to heroes from the Star Wars

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:23.690
Legends narrative.

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:28.460
The moon Trask is named for Trask Ulgo, a
character from the 2003 role-playing video

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:30.940
game Star Wars: Knights of the Old Republic.

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:35.480
Trask is an officer in the Galactic Republic
as well as a native of the doomed planet Alderaan.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:39.520
Kol Iben, on the other hand, is named for
a character from the world of comics: Kol

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:40.520
Skywalker.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:44.030
He's a Jedi descendant of both Anakin and
Luke who appears in the Dark Horse comic Star

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:45.760
Wars: Legacy.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:49.670
Chapter 12 sees the return of a number of
characters, including Horatio Sanz reprising

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:52.760
his role as the Mythrol, Mando's first target
in chapter one.

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:56.860
We also learn that it was Greef Karga who
put up the bounty on the amphibious alien.

00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:00.580
After Greef tells Mando that the Mythrol is
now working for him to pay off his debt, the

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:04.410
bounty hunter responds that he should let
him know if the alien tries to run away again.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.580
The Mythrol has a quick comeback to this suggestion

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:11.140
"Let me assure you, I do not wanna spend any
more time in carbonite.

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:12.730
Still can't see outta my left eye."

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:17.049
This is a callback to the finale of the original
Star Wars trilogy, Return of the Jedi.

00:04:17.049 --> 00:04:20.970
After being freed from carbonite in Jabba's
palace, the panicked Han Solo says he can't

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:25.000
see, and Leia explains that blindness is a
temporary side effect from being frozen in

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:26.000
carbonite.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:29.560
We don't know exactly how long it's been since
the Mythrol was freed from carbonite, but

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:32.190
it seems strange he would still suffer symptoms
from the freezing.

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:35.980
It could be he's fishing for sympathy, or
it could also be a ploy to put Greef off his

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:37.620
guard in hopes of a future escape.

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:40.540
After all, the Mythrol isn't known for his
honesty.

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:44.121
Even if he is telling the truth, there's a
good chance neither Greef, Cara, nor Mando

00:04:44.121 --> 00:04:47.740
believe him about his bad eye, since they
let him drive the speeder on their way to

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:49.240
the Imperial outpost.

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:53.410
As Mando and his companions are on their way
out of the Nevarro Imperial outpost, they

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:56.760
come across a lab filled with deformed bodies
floating in tanks.

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:00.140
In spite of their tight schedule, the heroes
pause to access records to find out what the

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:01.880
lab is actually all about.

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:06.150
Mythrol plays a holo-recording of Dr. Pershing,
the Imperial doctor who works on the child

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:07.550
in the first season.

00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:11.230
Among other things, Pershing reports, quote,
"There were promising effects for an entire

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:14.389
fortnight, but then, sadly, the body rejected
the blood.

00:05:14.389 --> 00:05:17.580
I highly doubt we'll find a donor with a higher
M-count though."

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:21.550
By "higher M-count," Dr. Pershing is referring
to one of the most controversial pieces of

00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:23.810
the Star Wars mythos: midi-chlorians.

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:27.040
In The Phantom Menace, we learn that every
Jedi's blood includes higher than average

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:31.050
amounts of midi-chlorians, and it's through
communication with these microscopic life

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:33.910
forms that Jedi find their special connection
to the Force.

00:05:33.910 --> 00:05:38.139
The fact that this concept still remains so
polarizing among some fans may be why Pershing

00:05:38.139 --> 00:05:42.130
says "M-count" rather than "midi-chlorian
count," allowing viewers on the thumbs-down

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:45.550
side of the debate to imagine "M" stands for
something else.

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:49.090
At the same time, the presence of the lab
and Pershing's recording have fed widespread

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:53.000
speculation that the experiments have something
to do with the cloning of Palpatine and the

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:55.740
creation of Supreme Leader Snoke.

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:59.950
Chapter 13 of The Mandalorian sees the long
anticipated live-action premiere of Ahsoka

00:05:59.950 --> 00:06:01.930
Tano, played by Rosario Dawson.

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:06.170
The former Jedi teams up with Mando against
the forces of Magistrate Morgan Elsbeth.

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:09.220
After some close calls, Ahsoka defeats the
magistrate in a duel.

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:12.730
Once she has Elsbeth at her mercy, Ahsoka
demands to know one thing

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:19.122
"Now tell me, where is your master?

00:06:19.122 --> 00:06:20.960
Where is Grand Admiral Thrawn?"

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:24.930
Thrawn is a villain too good to be left on
the cutting room floor of Star Wars canon.

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:28.990
The blue-skinned Imperial first appeared in
Timothy Zahn's Thrawn novel trilogy, starting

00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:34.020
with 1991's Heir to the Empire and concluding
with 1993's The Last Command.

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:37.980
While these novels have officially joined
the list of Star Wars media no longer considered

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:40.990
canon, Thrawn was also featured in Star Wars
Rebels.

00:06:40.990 --> 00:06:44.760
Ahsoka's lines let us know that Thrawn is
still alive and it's extremely important that

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:45.760
she finds him.

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:49.520
It could very well be that Ahsoka's hunt for
the admiral will be the focus of the upcoming

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:52.100
Disney Ahsoka Tano series.

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:57.010
Chapter 15 is another great example of just
how far Mando is willing to go to rescue Grogu.

00:06:57.010 --> 00:07:01.070
With the aid of the newly appointed New Republic
Marshall Cara Dune, Mando recruits former

00:07:01.070 --> 00:07:05.449
foe Migs Mayfeld to help him infiltrate an
Imperial facility in order to access a computer

00:07:05.449 --> 00:07:07.760
that can give him the location of Moff Gideon.

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:11.700
Once they're inside, Mayfeld refuses to approach
the computer console after spotting a former

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:13.430
commanding officer.

00:07:13.430 --> 00:07:17.110
Mando decides to do it himself, and makes
the surprising choice to remove a stormtrooper

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:20.169
helmet he's wearing as a disguise and expose
his face.

00:07:20.169 --> 00:07:24.480
When Mayfeld's old commander starts questioning
Mando, he swoops in to save the day, successfully

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:26.990
bluffing the officer into believing they're
the genuine article.

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:30.380
He also tries to make a quick work excuse
so they can get away from him

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:33.960
"C'mon, let's go fill out those TPS reports
so we can go recharge the power coils."

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:39.550
Mentioning T.P.S. reports is no doubt a reference
to the 1999 cult classic Office Space.

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:43.639
In the comedy, Ron Livingston plays Peter,
a passionless officer worker at a software

00:07:43.639 --> 00:07:49.290
company who is being hounded by his supervisor
Bill Lumbergh about his T.P.S. reports.

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:54.460
"Yeah, you see we're putting the cover sheets
on all T.P.S. reports now before they go out."

00:07:54.460 --> 00:07:58.669
Though decades old now, Office Space is often
still referenced and quoted by a wide variety

00:07:58.669 --> 00:08:01.780
of fans, even in a galaxy far, far away.

00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:05.910
In Chapter 15, Mayfeld reveals himself to
be a much more layered character than he originally

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:07.820
appears to be in the first season.

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:11.639
He and Mando are corralled into sharing drinks
with the Imperial officer Valin Hess, the

00:08:11.639 --> 00:08:13.430
same officer Mayfeld avoids earlier.

00:08:13.430 --> 00:08:17.600
We soon learn Hess holds an important place
in Mayfeld's history - one with such weight,

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:21.570
in fact, that Mayfeld allows it to overtake
his emotions and blow his cover.

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:26.060
As Hess asks for ideas for toasts, Mayfeld
suggests, "How about a toast to Operation:

00:08:26.060 --> 00:08:27.060
Cinder?"

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:29.830
Initially, Hess seems impressed by Mayfeld,
turning to Mando and saying, "Now there's

00:08:29.830 --> 00:08:31.550
a man who knows his history."

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:35.589
We soon learn Mayfeld served under Hess during
the operation, and was traumatized by what

00:08:35.589 --> 00:08:36.589
occurred.

00:08:36.589 --> 00:08:40.430
When you find out about Operation: Cinder,
his reaction is completely understandable.

00:08:40.430 --> 00:08:43.769
It wasn't something dreamed up specifically
for The Mandalorian, but instead has been

00:08:43.769 --> 00:08:47.290
mentioned in numerous Star Wars video games,
comics, and other media.

00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:51.110
Cinder is Palpatine's contingency plan in
the case of his death, to make sure the galaxy's

00:08:51.110 --> 00:08:53.300
civilizations don't outlive him.

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:57.670
Weeks after the Battle of Endor, the Empire
set Operation: Cinder in motion, using satellite

00:08:57.670 --> 00:09:01.959
arrays to cause disastrous climate changes
on different planets including Naboo.

00:09:01.959 --> 00:09:06.010
No official number has ever been given for
the amount of deaths, but it was clearly significant

00:09:06.010 --> 00:09:09.690
enough to make Mayfeld shoot Hess for his
involvement.

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:13.900
Early in chapter 16, Mando returns to Trask
with allies, hoping to enlist the help of

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:15.379
the Mandalorians he found there.

00:09:15.379 --> 00:09:19.980
Bo-Katan and Koska are not happy to see Mando
accompanied by Boba, whose voice they recognize

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:22.129
as those of the clones created on Kamino.

00:09:22.129 --> 00:09:26.850
Koska and Boba exchange a few barbs, eventually
leading to a brief altercation.

00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:30.261
Before things get violent, Boba warns the
younger Koska to calm down.

00:09:30.261 --> 00:09:33.459
She responds by saying, "You'll be talking
through the window of a bacta tank."

00:09:33.459 --> 00:09:37.250
Bacta tanks aren't a particularly obscure
part of Star Wars lore, though you don't often

00:09:37.250 --> 00:09:38.990
hear them referred to by name.

00:09:38.990 --> 00:09:42.940
They're cylindrical tanks filled with bacta
fluid, which is known for its healing properties.

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:46.980
Probably the most prominent use of a bacta
tank comes early in Empire Strikes Back when

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:50.920
Luke Skywalker is submerged in one of the
tanks after being rescued on the planet Hoth

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:52.360
by Han Solo.

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:54.699
